WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.022
This lesson becomes much easier once current is treated as a flow rate.

2
00:00:05.022 --> 00:00:10.044
Charge is the quantity being moved.

3
00:00:10.044 --> 00:00:15.065
Current tells how quickly that charge passes a point in the circuit.

4
00:00:15.065 --> 00:00:20.087
So current is not a store of charge, and it is not a one-time packet.

5
00:00:20.087 --> 00:00:25.109
It is the per-second rate of charge flow through the loop.

6
00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:30.131
That meaning matters because the formula only makes sense after the learner can picture charge moving through a wire and crossing a chosen point in time.

7
00:00:30.131 --> 00:00:35.153
Keep this takeaway in view: Current is the rate of charge flow, not the charge itself.

8
00:00:35.153 --> 00:00:40.175
Charge means what moves.

9
00:00:40.175 --> 00:00:45.196
Charge is the electrical quantity carried through the circuit.

10
00:00:45.196 --> 00:00:50.218
Current means rate of flow.

11
00:00:50.218 --> 00:00:55.240
Current tells how much charge passes a point each second.

12
00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:02.138
Imagine drawing a tiny gate on the wire and counting how much charge crosses that gate in one second.

13
00:01:02.138 --> 00:01:09.035
That is the picture behind current.

14
00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:15.932
The charges keep moving around the closed loop, and current is the crossing rate at that chosen point.

15
00:01:15.932 --> 00:01:22.830
The diagram matters because it stops students from thinking that current gets used up before the charge reaches the next component.

16
00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:29.727
In a simple loop, the same rate of flow passes every point because charge cannot pile up at one place and disappear at another.

17
00:01:29.727 --> 00:01:36.625
Keep this takeaway in view: Read current as crossing rate through a point on the loop.

18
00:01:36.625 --> 00:01:43.523
Point marker: Pick one point on the loop and imagine counting charge crossing there.

19
00:01:43.523 --> 00:01:50.420
Closed loop: Charge keeps circulating, so the flow must stay continuous.

20
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.987
A useful analogy is water moving through a pipe.

21
00:01:54.987 --> 00:01:59.553
The total water is like charge.

22
00:01:59.553 --> 00:02:04.120
The litres passing a point each second are like current.

23
00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:08.687
That comparison helps because it separates the thing that moves from the rate at which it moves.

24
00:02:08.687 --> 00:02:13.253
It also helps explain why one narrow point in the story does not destroy the flow rate idea.

25
00:02:13.253 --> 00:02:17.820
In a steady closed loop, the flow rate through one point and the next point stays matched.

26
00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:22.387
The analogy is not perfect, but it gives the learner a stable image before the symbols appear.

27
00:02:22.387 --> 00:02:26.953
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the distinction clear: charge is the quantity, current is the rate.

28
00:02:26.953 --> 00:02:31.520
Charge means water amount.

29
00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.087
The quantity being transported around the system.

30
00:02:36.087 --> 00:02:40.653
Current means flow per second.

31
00:02:40.653 --> 00:02:45.220
How much of that quantity passes a point each second.

32
00:02:45.220 --> 00:02:50.083
The formula for current is I equals Q divided by t.

33
00:02:50.083 --> 00:02:54.947
Read that in words as current equals charge divided by time.

34
00:02:54.947 --> 00:02:59.810
It is saying exactly what the lesson has been describing: current is how much charge passes in each second or over any stated time interval.

35
00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:04.673
If more charge passes in the same time, the current is larger.

36
00:03:04.673 --> 00:03:09.537
If the same charge takes longer to pass, the current is smaller.

37
00:03:09.537 --> 00:03:14.400
The symbol rule is short, but its meaning is the full flow story you already built first.

38
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.263
Keep this takeaway in view: I = Q / t is just the rate-of-flow idea written compactly.

39
00:03:19.263 --> 00:03:24.127
Bigger Q: More charge in the same time means a bigger current.

40
00:03:24.127 --> 00:03:28.990
Bigger t: The same charge spread over longer time means a smaller current.

41
00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:33.853
Q = It.

42
00:03:33.853 --> 00:03:38.717
Use charge passed and time taken.

43
00:03:38.717 --> 00:03:43.580
Then read the answer as a flow rate.

44
00:03:43.580 --> 00:03:48.976
For a simple closed series loop, the current is the same at every point.

45
00:03:48.976 --> 00:03:54.373
The reason is continuity of charge flow.

46
00:03:54.373 --> 00:03:59.769
If 6 coulombs per second pass one point in the loop, then 6 coulombs per second must pass the next point as well.

47
00:03:59.769 --> 00:04:05.165
Otherwise charge would have to build up somewhere in the wire or vanish from it.

48
00:04:05.165 --> 00:04:10.562
This does not mean every quantity stays the same in the loop.

49
00:04:10.562 --> 00:04:15.958
Voltage can be shared, but the current in one unbranched loop stays common all the way around.

50
00:04:15.958 --> 00:04:21.355
Keep this takeaway in view: One simple loop means one shared current all the way around.

51
00:04:21.355 --> 00:04:26.751
Series loop means same current.

52
00:04:26.751 --> 00:04:32.147
One path means one common rate of charge flow.

53
00:04:32.147 --> 00:04:37.544
Why means continuity.

54
00:04:37.544 --> 00:04:42.940
Charge cannot quietly accumulate at one point and disappear at another.

55
00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:49.798
A very common trap is to imagine that current gets used up by the lamp, so less current reaches the rest of the circuit.

56
00:04:49.798 --> 00:04:56.655
That is not how a simple series loop behaves.

57
00:04:56.655 --> 00:05:03.512
The lamp transfers energy from the charges, but the rate of charge flow through the loop stays the same.

58
00:05:03.512 --> 00:05:10.370
The battery provides the push around the circuit, and the components transfer energy, but in one unbranched path the same current passes each point.

59
00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:17.228
Separating current from energy transfer prevents a lot of later confusion.

60
00:05:17.228 --> 00:05:24.085
Keep this takeaway in view: Energy can be transferred in a component without the current being used up.

61
00:05:24.085 --> 00:05:30.942
Current is charge flow rate, not stored charge.

62
00:05:30.942 --> 00:05:37.800
A lamp can transfer energy without using up the current itself.

63
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.487
Try three checks.

64
00:05:41.487 --> 00:05:45.174
First, what does current measure?

65
00:05:45.174 --> 00:05:48.861
The rate of charge flow past a point.

66
00:05:48.861 --> 00:05:52.548
Second, what quantity appears in the current formula besides time?

67
00:05:52.548 --> 00:05:56.235
Charge passed.

68
00:05:56.235 --> 00:05:59.922
Third, in one simple unbranched loop, what happens to the current at different points?

69
00:05:59.922 --> 00:06:03.608
It stays the same.

70
00:06:03.608 --> 00:06:07.295
Those three ideas are the core of the lesson, and the formal example simply turns them into numbers.

71
00:06:07.295 --> 00:06:10.982
Keep this takeaway in view: If the flow story is clear, the current calculation becomes straightforward.

72
00:06:10.982 --> 00:06:14.669
Meaning means flow rate.

73
00:06:14.669 --> 00:06:18.356
Current tells how much charge crosses a point per second.

74
00:06:18.356 --> 00:06:22.043
Formula partner means charge.

75
00:06:22.043 --> 00:06:25.730
The calculation uses charge passed and time taken.

76
00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:31.772
You are ready for the worked example now.

77
00:06:31.772 --> 00:06:37.814
If 18 coulombs pass a point in 3 seconds, current means charge per second, so use I equals Q divided by t.

78
00:06:37.814 --> 00:06:43.856
Eighteen divided by three gives 6 amperes.

79
00:06:43.856 --> 00:06:49.898
Read that answer properly: 6 coulombs of charge pass the chosen point each second.

80
00:06:49.898 --> 00:06:55.940
And because this is one simple series loop, that same 6 ampere current passes every point around the loop, including before and after the lamp.

81
00:06:55.940 --> 00:07:01.982
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: use the flow-rate meaning, then calculate cleanly.

82
00:07:01.982 --> 00:07:08.024
Step 1: Identify charge passed and time taken.

83
00:07:08.024 --> 00:07:14.066
Step 2: Use I = Q / t to calculate the current.

84
00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:20.108
I = 18 / 3.

85
00:07:20.108 --> 00:07:26.150
Read 6 A as 6 coulombs of charge passing a point each second.
